  "query_preview": "..(Tuesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 353 PM EDT SUN AUG 16 2026\n\nAt the onset of the long term period, a strong closed upper level\nlow will sit over Manitoba, with general troughing over the \nnorthern CONUS, especially in the vicinity of the Great Lakes. \nMeanwhile, a relatively flattened ridge will linger over the \nsouthern US, with eastern Kentucky finding itself in a region of \nzonal to slightly northwesterly flow aloft, depending on the \norientation of ridging. At the surface, the previous frontal \nsystem will be south of the forecast area, having fully moved \nthrough, and surface high pressure will be building near the \nMiddle Mississippi Valley. Behind the surface high, a secondary \ncold front associated with the relatively vertically stacked \nCanadian low will extend southward into the Upper Midwest. As \nthese features travel eastward, building high pressure over the \nforecast area will keep eastern Kentucky largely dry on Tuesday. \nTemperatures will be fairly seasonable as well, with highs ranging\nfrom the mid to upper 80s across the forecast area. Clear skies \novernight may allow a very modest ridge/valley temperature split \nto develop, with the limiting factor being lingering moisture. MOS\nguidance is rather aggressive with these splits, but given \nexpected moisture, valley minTs have been nudged down only \nmodestly into the low 60s for now. Of note, fog formation on \nWednesday morning appears likely for much of the forecast area, \nparticularly in typical mainstem river valleys.\n\nDry weather will persist through much of Wednesday as surface \nhigh pressure continues to translate through the area. Meanwhile, \nthe upper level trough will continue to dig towards the \nCommonwealth, with the aforementioned cold front pushing \nsoutheast. By Wednesday evening, the front will approach eastern \nKentucky and rain chances will return to the area. Of note, some \nmodels depict an additional shortwave traversing the base of the \ntrough (roughly centered over the Ohio Valley) around the time \nfrontally forced convection approaches, or perhaps slightly \nbefore. This enhanced mid level flow may elevate shear somewhat, \nand depending on frontal timing, instability may also be adequate \nfor stronger storms, although model spread remains quite high at \nthis time. Any risk for severe weather (or lack thereof) remains \ndependent on how these ingredients line up, with overall risk \nlooking quite low at this time. Meanwhile, a moist air mass will \nbe in place ahead of the front, with LREF mean PW values generally\nin the range of 1.6-1.7 in (roughly 80th percentile or higher \ncompared to climatology). This, in addition to potentially \nenhanced convective coverage if shortwave energy interacts with \nthe front, could allow for at least isolated instances of flash \nflooding. Again, model variability remains very high at this \nstage, but WPC has already outlined the forecast area in a Day 4 \nMarginal (Level 1/4) Risk for Excessive Rainfall on Wednesday, and\nan additional Day 5 Marginal Risk on Thursday as the system \nsags south. Guidance does suggest the front will slow as it \npasses through the southern portion of our area and into \nTennessee. Exactly how quickly this boundary progresses is still a\nsource of uncertainty, but rain chances have been maintained \nthrough Thursday to account for this slowing motion. Any \nhydrological concerns will also be dependent on the speed of this \nfrontal passage in addition to the extent of convective coverage.\n\nFollowing the frontal passage, troughing will continue to \namplify, eventually digging all the way into the Southeast as \nridging retreats towards the Four Corners region. This will usher \nin cooler weather, with high temperatures as low as the upper 70s \nfor some locations on Friday (and perhaps lower if convection can \ntake hold). Additional impulses will keep at least scattered \nshowers and storms in the forecast each day, with rain chances \ngenerally peaking in the afternoon/evening.",
